<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<!--
  werust's Win32 application manifest (task `windows-release-packaging-leg`).

  EDITING THESE COMMENTS: a double hyphen is ILLEGAL inside an XML comment (XML
  1.0 §2.5), so this repo's ordinary prose dash cannot be used here. Rephrase
  with a comma, a colon, parentheses or two sentences. `mt.exe` refuses a
  malformed manifest at LINK time on the Windows runner, which no Ubuntu gate can
  reach, so `crates/werust-core/tests/release_plumbing_shape.rs` parses this file
  instead (task `windows-app-manifest-is-malformed-xml-and-breaks-the-release-link`).

  It is EMBEDDED into every binary this crate produces by `build.rs`, which hands
  it to the MSVC linker as `/MANIFEST:EMBED /MANIFESTINPUT:<this file>`. Decisions
  (including why that mechanism rather than a resource compiler):
  docs/spikes/windows-release-packaging-leg/README.md.

  It carries exactly two declarations, both of which the window has been waiting
  for since `windows-win32-window-and-chrome` deliberately shipped without a
  manifest (that spike's DECISIONS.md, section 4):

  1. THE COMMON-CONTROLS v6 DEPENDENCY. Windows ships comctl32 5.82 AND 6; a
     process gets 6 only by asking for it here. Without it every control draws
     in the pre-Vista style. The identity below is Microsoft's documented one
     (name + version + publicKeyToken + language). A wrong token silently leaves
     the process on 5.82 rather than failing, which is why the shape test pins
     all three.

     What it does NOT buy: dark mode. Standard Win32 controls do not follow the
     OS light/dark setting on the strength of this manifest; that needs the
     undocumented uxtheme path or owner-drawn controls, so the chrome's push
     BUTTONs stay light in dark mode and the `follow-os-color-scheme` parity cell
     stays `stubbed`. Ground truth + sources:
     work/notes/findings/win32-common-controls-dark-mode-needs-more-than-a-v6-manifest-2026-07-31.md.

  2. PER-MONITOR-V2 DPI AWARENESS. Without it Windows bitmap-scales the whole
     process on a 150%/200% display (blurry chrome AND a blurry page). Both
     spellings are present because they are read by different Windows versions:
     `<dpiAwareness>` is Windows 10 1607+ and wins where it is understood, and
     `<dpiAware>` is the pre-1607 fallback (Microsoft's own documented pairing,
     "Setting the default DPI awareness for a process"). `true/pm` is per-monitor
     v1, the closest thing that older Windows can express.

     The consequence the by-hand check must judge: `crates/werust-windows/src/chrome.rs`
     lays its rectangles out in RAW pixels, so a DPI-aware process draws the
     chrome at 100% metrics on a 200% display (crisp, but small), where an
     unaware one drew it blurry at the right apparent size. The PAGE is the half
     that unambiguously improves. Recorded at
     work/notes/observations/windows-chrome-is-raw-pixels-under-per-monitor-v2-2026-07-31.md.

  There is deliberately NO <trustInfo>/<requestedExecutionLevel> here: the MSVC
  linker adds the default `asInvoker` fragment when it embeds this, which is what
  werust wants (a browser must never ask for elevation).
-->
<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0">
  <!--
    The side-by-side identity of the application itself. Its `version` is a
    four-part Win32 assembly version, NOT werust's version: werust's ONE version
    is `werust_core::version()`, resolved from the release tag by
    crates/werust-core/build.rs and compiled into the binary. Wiring this field
    to it would mint the second version source this repo keeps removing, and
    could not represent it anyway (`0.2.9-3-gabc1234` is not `n.n.n.n`).
  -->
  <assemblyIdentity
      type="win32"
      name="wighawag.werust.werust-windows"
      version="1.0.0.0"
      processorArchitecture="*"
  />
  <description>werust, a from-scratch web browser for a post-trusted-server web.</description>

  <dependency>
    <dependentAssembly>
      <assemblyIdentity
          type="win32"
          name="Microsoft.Windows.Common-Controls"
          version="6.0.0.0"
          processorArchitecture="*"
          publicKeyToken="6595b64144ccf1df"
          language="*"
      />
    </dependentAssembly>
  </dependency>

  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <dpiAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2005/WindowsSettings">true/pm</dpiAware>
      <dpiAwareness xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">PerMonitorV2</dpiAwareness>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
</assembly>
